From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 19:50:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: repair text subtitles as they are read
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Text subtitles reach the screen with damage no one meant: UTF-8 read as
Windows-1252 ("â™ª" where a music note belongs, "Ã©" for "é"), notes that
an OCR turned into "¶" or "#", letters it misread ("wouId", "lnspector"),
advertising and uploader credits, and lines that end before they start or
run into the next one. Across one library of 821 SubRip files, 538 carried
at least one of these.

Repair them as the subtitle is read, in memory: a SubRip file in
CDVDSubtitleStream, and each cue of a SubRip or plain-text track inside
the video in CDVDOverlayCodecText. The file on disk is never touched and
the player lists the subtitle once, as before.

Each rule fixes one known kind of damage and nothing else. The words, the
formatting tags and the start times are never changed; an end time moves
only when the line cannot be seen or makes the dialogue jump. A file that
is not UTF-8 is read in the code page of the language its name gives, and
one whose name gives none and does not read as Western text is left to
Kodi. A character set the user chose still wins.

Misread letters are undone by the subtitle's own pattern, in any language
a pack is installed for. An OCR repeats the same misreadings all through
a subtitle - one font, one engine - and a clean subtitle has none. So the
subtitle first shows which it makes: a word that is not a word of its
language, and that one confusion ("l" for "I", "cl" for "d", "rn" for
"m", "0" for "O", a dropped accent) turns into exactly one word, is
evidence, and a confusion counts only when it is frequent - a real share
of how the subtitle writes that letter, or across many words. Only those
are undone, only into a word, only in a line of the subtitle's language.
An exception among correct uses, a name used mid-sentence, a line in
another language, an accent on a word another language has, numbers and
Roman numerals are left as they are. A track inside the video learns as
its cues come.

The language is the one the file name or the track gives or, when neither
does, CLD2's reading of the text when it is sure. A pack is a language's
Hunspell dictionaries and a short profile, installed as a library add-on
carrying resources/subtitlerepair/<language>/. None is built in: every
installed pack is held in memory, so Repair > Languages lists the packs
the repositories offer and installs the ones chosen, uninstalling those
left out, and the packs in use are read again when that changes.
Hunspell and CLD2 are optional dependencies: without them the other
repairs still run.

CDVDStreamInfo now carries the language the container declares for a
stream, so the text codec knows a track's language. Nothing else reads
it; the codec cannot reach the demuxer stream any other way.

Add Player > Subtitles > Repair, with advertising, misread letters and
broken timing as separate options, all on, and the languages to correct
letters in, none until chosen. Its option filler and callback register in
CSettings beside the others.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
